                "text": "**Analyzing the Transformation**\n\nOkay, I'm trying to figure out this transformation. The input size is 7x8, and zeros become fives (gray). It's the non-zero rows that are key. I noticed that with the shift left, the values of the rows don't shift perfectly left. There's some other process happening, too!\n\n\n**Investigating the Shift**\n\nIt seems clear now. Non-zero values shift left by one, and if they start at the leftmost column, they disappear entirely! The length of the non-zero sequence dictates the length in the output. Zeros become fives, of course. I've got it - a simple left shift and value replacement!\n\n\n**Confirming the Formula**\n\nI've got a concise formula now, I believe! `output[r][c] = input[r][c+1]` if `(c+1 < width and input[r][c+1] != 0)` else 5. The rightmost column always becomes 5.  I've verified it against the given examples; it matches perfectly.\n\n\n**Validating the Pattern**\n\nI'm now running the formula I established on a new, larger test input (10x10), and the outputs continue to align perfectly with the expected transformation, reinforcing my confidence. Every row is handled correctly. This confirms the robustness of my understanding of how the zeros and non-zeros interact.\n\n\n",
